  "body": "## A column with no producer, finally wired\n\nThe `headers` column has existed since the original request-logging commit in **July** — schema, dataclass field, INSERT, and a JSON decode on every detail read — and has been **NULL on every one of 142,718 rows**, because nothing ever set it.\n\nThis supplies the missing producer. The column, field type, INSERT and read path are untouched; they were already correct.\n\n## The filter is positive, and that is the whole design\n\nHeaders carry `authorization` and `x-api-key` on **every single request**. So the allow-list lives in a neutral module that is the only place deciding which header values may be persisted:\n\n```\nuser-agent · x-app · anthropic-version · anthropic-beta · accept · content-type\n```\n\nA deny-list was rejected outright: it is wrong the moment a client sends a header nobody anticipated — and discovering what clients actually send is the point of this data.\n\n**Unlisted headers get their NAMES recorded and their values never read**, under a single reserved entry:\n\n```json\n\"(unlisted)\": \"authorization,cookie,proxy-authorization,x-api-key\"\n```\n\nParentheses are **illegal in an RFC 9110 field name**, so that key cannot collide with a real header. Names are sanitised to the token character set, which structurally strips colons, whitespace and quotes — so value text cannot ride along inside the list *even if the code above it changed*. You can see that a client sent `x-api-key` without the key ever touching the database.\n\n## Where the headers come from\n\n**Explicitly, from the FastAPI endpoints** — they already take `Request` for `get_request_id`, so `request.headers` was one parameter away, threaded down the same chain that already carries the request id. No ContextVar, no middleware change, no new global state. Every new parameter defaults to `None`, so existing call sites and tests compile unchanged. Four files, +33/−6.\n\n## Caps\n\n| Cap | Value | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| value chars | 1024 | a real user-agent plus a full `anthropic-beta` list is well under 1 KiB |\n| unlisted names | 32 | overflow appends `+N-more`, so a count is never silently lost |\n| name chars | 64 | longest real field names are ~30 |\n| total chars | 4096 | overflow drops the discretionary `(unlisted)` entry first, then sheds **whole** entries — every surviving value is a faithful copy, not a truncated one |\n\n## Verification\n\n**The leak test tries to leak.** It builds a request carrying `authorization`, `x-api-key`, `cookie`, `proxy-authorization` and a bearer token, runs the real capture path, and asserts the **entire serialised row** contains none of those values — the whole row, not the fields someone remembered to check, because a field-level assertion has missed a real leak in this repo before. It also asserts the *names* do appear, so removing discovery later is a visible decision rather than a silent one.\n\nMutation-tested:\n\n```\ninvert allow-list into a deny-list  -> 11 failed, incl. test_no_secret_value_reaches_the_stored_row\nremove value suppression            ->  7 failed, incl. the same leak test\n```\n\nIndependent probe with five secret-bearing headers:\n\n```\ncaptured: {\"user-agent\": \"...\", \"anthropic-version\": \"...\", \"anthropic-beta\": \"...\",\n           \"accept\": \"...\", \"(unlisted)\": \"authorization,cookie,proxy-authorization,x-api-key,x-custom-token\"}\nsecret VALUES present : NONE\nsecret FRAGMENTS      : NONE\nnames discoverable    : True\n```\n\n```\nfull suite (clean shell)  4792 passed, 6 pre-existing PowerShell failures\nruff format / ruff check / ty check  clean repo-wide\n```\n\n## Residual risks\n\n**~28 MB across a 142k-row log** at roughly 200 bytes per row. Small against the current 800 MB, but it is new steady-state growth on a table that has had a whole arc spent shrinking it.\n\n**Old rows stay NULL.** No backfill is possible — the headers were never captured.\n\n**The allow-list is a judgement call.** It captures client identity and protocol version, nothing else. If a header turns out to matter later it must be added deliberately, which is the intended cost of a positive list.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti\n",
