    "message": "feat(analytics): capture inbound request headers behind a positive allow-list\n\nThe `headers` column has existed since the original request-logging commit\nin July -- schema, dataclass field, INSERT, and a JSON decode on every\ndetail read -- and has been NULL on every one of 142,718 rows, because\nnothing ever set it. A column with no producer. This supplies the\nproducer.\n\nHeaders carry `authorization` and `x-api-key` on every single request, so\nthe filter is a POSITIVE allow-list in a neutral module that is the only\nplace deciding which header values may be persisted:\n\n    user-agent, x-app, anthropic-version, anthropic-beta, accept,\n    content-type\n\nA deny-list was rejected outright: it is wrong the moment a client sends\na header nobody anticipated, and the whole point of this data is to\ndiscover what clients actually send.\n\nHeaders that arrive but are not allow-listed have their NAMES recorded\nand their values never read, under a single reserved `(unlisted)` entry.\nParentheses are illegal in an RFC 9110 field name, so that key cannot\ncollide with a real header, and the names are sanitised down to the token\ncharacter set -- which structurally strips colons, whitespace and quotes,\nso value text cannot ride along inside the list even if the code above it\nchanged. You can see that a client sent `x-api-key` without the key ever\ntouching the database.\n\nRead explicitly from the FastAPI endpoints, which already take the\nRequest for `get_request_id`, and threaded down the same call chain that\nalready carries the request id. No ContextVar, no middleware change, no\nnew global state. Every new parameter defaults to None, so existing call\nsites and tests compile unchanged.\n\nFour caps bound a hostile or odd client without touching real traffic:\n1024 chars per value, 32 unlisted names (overflow appends `+N-more` so a\ncount is never silently lost), 64 chars per name, 4096 chars total --\noverflow drops the discretionary `(unlisted)` entry first, then sheds\nwhole entries, so every value that survives is a faithful copy rather\nthan a truncated one.\n\nVerification:\n- The leak test builds a request carrying authorization, x-api-key,\n  cookie, proxy-authorization and a bearer token, runs the real capture\n  path, and asserts the ENTIRE serialised row contains none of those\n  values -- the whole row, not the fields someone remembered to check,\n  because a field-level assertion has missed a real leak here before. It\n  also asserts the names DO appear, so removing discovery would be a\n  visible decision rather than a silent one.\n- Mutation-tested: inverting the allow-list into a deny-list failed 11\n  tests including the leak test by name; removing value suppression for\n  unlisted headers failed 7, again including the leak test.\n- Independently probed with five secret-bearing headers: no value and no\n  fragment of one appears in the captured row, while every name does.\n- Full suite on a clean shell: 4792 passed, 6 pre-existing PowerShell\n  installer failures.\n- ruff format, ruff check, ty check clean repo-wide.\n\nNot touched: the column, the dataclass field type, the INSERT, or the\nread path. They were already correct; only the producer was missing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
